---
/**
 * /alpha-line-report — the citable version of everything we know about the Alpha
 * line, in the shape this kind of page has to take to be worth citing.
 *
 * The shape is borrowed from the build reports other directories publish
 * (madewithjev.com/jev-statistics is the reference we looked at on 2026-09-22,
 * teardown in docs/research/): a dated dataset, a method section, an explicit
 * statement of what it cannot tell you, and a licence that says "quote this".
 *
 * It is not a second register. /stealth-models is the register — one row per
 * codename, the fields a reader checks. This page is the arithmetic on those
 * rows, plus the dates that only make sense in aggregate, and it exists because
 * "how long does a stealth model stay anonymous" has an answer now that it did
 * not have in March.
 */
import System from "../../layouts/System.astro";
import JsonLd from "../../components/JsonLd.astro";
import Faq from "../../components/Faq.astro";
import Provenance from "../../components/Provenance.astro";
import { articleSchema, breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, SITE, SITE_NAME } from "../../lib/schema";
import {
  ALPHA_LINE_CITATION,
  ALPHA_LINE_LICENSE,
  alphaLineEntries,
  alphaLineStats,
  daysBetween,
} from "@repo/lib/alpha-line";

const title = "OpenRouter Stealth Models: 3 Codenames, Dated and Resolved (2026)";
const description =
  "Every anonymous Alpha-line release on OpenRouter, with the dates: 3 codenames, a median of 6 days from codename to the maker's own listing, all three free while anonymous and none still free. Dated, sourced and free to cite.";
const canonical = `${SITE}/alpha-line-report`;

const stats = alphaLineStats;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How many stealth models has OpenRouter had?",
    answer: `We can document ${stats.count}: ${alphaLineEntries.map((entry) => entry.codename).join(", ")}. That is a floor, not a total — a codename we cannot tie to a public listing before and after is left out rather than guessed at, and the old codename disappears from the catalogue at the reveal, which makes later verification impossible.`,
  },
  {
    question: "How long does a stealth model stay anonymous?",
    answer: `Across the three we can date, the gap from the codename appearing to the maker's own model id appearing was ${stats.windows.join(", ")} days — a median of ${stats.medianWindowDays}. The range is the useful part: the fastest was ${stats.fastestWindowDays} day and the slowest ${stats.slowestWindowDays}.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Do stealth models stay free?",
    answer: `No. All ${stats.freeWhileAnonymous} were free while anonymous and none is free now (${stats.stillFreeAfterReveal} of ${stats.freeWhileAnonymous}). The free window is a trial that ends at or before the reveal, and it ends without notice — that is the operational fact to plan around, not the price.`,
  },
  {
    question: "What did the codenames turn out to be?",
    answer: alphaLineEntries
      .map((entry) => `${entry.codename} was ${entry.became}`)
      .join("; ") + ". All three are documented from the OpenRouter catalogue, which is where the codenames appeared in the first place.",
  },
  {
    question: "How much do the revealed models cost now?",
    answer: `Between $${stats.inputPriceFloor} and $${stats.inputPriceCeiling} per million input tokens — a spread of ${stats.priceSpreadFactor}x. The codename tells you nothing about the price you will pay afterwards, which is the strongest argument for keeping a fallback configured while you evaluate one.`,
  },
  {
    question: "How is this different from the stealth models index?",
    answer:
      "/stealth-models is the register: one row per codename with the fields a reader re-checks in the catalogue. This page is the arithmetic over those rows — window lengths, cadence, and what the reveals did to the prices — plus the method and the licence. Both are dated; neither is generated from the other.",
  },
];
---
<System title={`${title} | ${SITE_NAME}`} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={articleSchema({
      title: "The Alpha Line Report",
      description,
      publishedAt: stats.dataAsOf,
      updatedAt: stats.dataAsOf,
      image: `${SITE}/og-image.png`,
      url: canonical,
    })}
  />
  <JsonLd slot="head" data={faqSchema(faqs)} />
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={breadcrumbSchema([
      { name: "Home", url: SITE },
      { name: "Stealth models", url: `${SITE}/stealth-models` },
      { name: "The Alpha Line Report", url: canonical },
    ])}
  />

  <p class="kicker">Report · {stats.dataAsOf}</p>
  <h1 class="display">The Alpha Line Report</h1>
  <div class="byline">
    <span>Register of {stats.count} codenames</span>
    <span>Median {stats.medianWindowDays} days to the maker's listing</span>
    <span>Licence {ALPHA_LINE_LICENSE}</span>
  </div>
  <p class="lede">
    OpenRouter's anonymous releases are a pattern, not an accident: a frontier-class model appears with
    no maker and no price, gets claimed and renamed weeks later, and the codename is deleted. This page
    is the arithmetic over the three we can date — how long each stayed anonymous and what the reveal
    did to the price.
  </p>

  <section id="in-short">
    <h2>In short</h2>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="label">Codenames documented</div>
        <div class="value">{stats.count}</div>
        <div class="sub">before and after, tied to a listing</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="label">Median days anonymous</div>
        <div class="value">{stats.medianWindowDays}</div>
        <div class="sub">{stats.windows.join(", ")} days across the three</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="label">Still free</div>
        <div class="value">0 of {stats.freeWhileAnonymous}</div>
        <div class="sub">all were free while anonymous</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="label">Price after the reveal</div>
        <div class="value">{stats.priceSpreadFactor}x</div>
        <div class="sub">${stats.inputPriceFloor} to ${stats.inputPriceCeiling} per M input</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="measure">
      The codename is not a discount tier and never was. Every model in the line was free while
      anonymous and paid afterwards, and the price afterwards varies by a factor of
      {" "}{stats.priceSpreadFactor} between the cheapest and the most expensive — so the useful planning
      assumption is not "it will be cheap", it is "it will be priced by someone else, soon, without
      notice".
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="register">
    <h2>The three, with dates</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Codename</th>
            <th>Listed</th>
            <th>Maker's listing</th>
            <th class="num">Days</th>
            <th>Turned out to be</th>
            <th class="num">Now, per M in</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {alphaLineEntries.map((entry) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={entry.href}>{entry.codename}</a></td>
              <td style="color: var(--text-muted);">{entry.firstListed}</td>
              <td style="color: var(--text-muted);">{entry.productAdded}</td>
              <td class="num"><strong>{daysBetween(entry.firstListed, entry.productAdded)}</strong></td>
              <td style="color: var(--text-muted);">{entry.became}</td>
              <td class="num">${entry.inputPricePerMillion}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Provenance
      source="OpenRouter catalogue reads, dated in the register"
      verified={stats.dataAsOf}
      extra={[
        "Listed = the day the codename appeared; maker's listing = the catalogue's own created date for the model id that replaced it",
      ]}
    />
    <p class="footnote">
      Two of these dates are different events, and Hunter Alpha is why the table shows both: the identity
      was disclosed on {alphaLineEntries[0].disclosed}, but the listing under Xiaomi's own name appeared
      on {alphaLineEntries[0].productAdded} — {daysBetween(
        alphaLineEntries[0].disclosed as string,
        alphaLineEntries[0].productAdded,
      )} days later. A report that published one "reveal date" per codename would have hidden that.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cadence">
    <h2>How often they arrive</h2>
    <p class="measure">
      Two gaps so far: {stats.gaps[0].days} days between {stats.gaps[0].from} and {stats.gaps[0].to}, then
      {" "}{stats.gaps[1].days} days between {stats.gaps[1].from} and {stats.gaps[1].to}. Two intervals is
      not a cadence, and we are not going to fit a curve to it — but the direction is the thing to watch,
      because a reader deciding whether to build tooling for the next codename is really asking whether
      there will be a next codename soon.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="price">
    <h2>What the reveal did to the price</h2>
    <div class="grid-cards">
      {alphaLineEntries.map((entry) => (
        <article class="card">
          <div class="reveal-head">
            <h3 style="margin: 0;">{entry.codename} → {entry.became}</h3>
            <span>{daysBetween(entry.firstListed, entry.productAdded)} days</span>
          </div>
          <p style="margin: var(--space-2) 0 0; color: var(--text-muted); font-size: var(--text-sm);">
            {entry.priceStory}
          </p>
          <p style="margin: var(--space-2) 0 0; color: var(--text-faint); font-size: var(--text-xs);">
            ${entry.inputPricePerMillion} in / ${entry.outputPricePerMillion} out per million tokens
            {entry.cachedInputPricePerMillion
              ? `, $${entry.cachedInputPricePerMillion} cached`
              : ""}
            {" · "}{entry.contextWindow.toLocaleString("en-US")} token window
          </p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="cannot">
    <h2>What this report cannot tell you</h2>
    <ul class="measure" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
      <li>
        <strong style="color: var(--text);">Whether three is all of them.</strong> It is what we can tie
        to a listing before and after. A codename we only saw in a forum post is not evidence, and after
        a reveal the old id is gone from the catalogue, so we cannot go back and check.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong style="color: var(--text);">When the makers decided anything.</strong> Every date here is
        either the day a listing appeared or the day a disclosure was published. The decision behind it
        happened earlier and is not observable.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong style="color: var(--text);">Whether the next one follows the pattern.</strong>
        {" "}{stats.count} observations, three of them, from one vendor each. The pattern is real enough
        to plan around and far too small to predict from.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong style="color: var(--text);">That the revealed model is the same thing you tested.</strong>
        Two of the three changed price and one changed window between the anonymous listing and the
        product listing. Whether the weights changed is not public.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="quote">
    <h2>Facts you can quote, already written</h2>
    <ul class="measure" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
      <li>
        We can document <strong style="color: var(--text);">{stats.count} anonymous releases</strong> on
        OpenRouter in 2026: Hunter Alpha, OX Alpha and Union Alpha.
      </li>
      <li>
        Median <strong style="color: var(--text);">{stats.medianWindowDays} days</strong> from codename to
        the maker's own listing ({stats.windows.join(", ")} days).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong style="color: var(--text);">{stats.freeWhileAnonymous} of {stats.freeWhileAnonymous}</strong>{" "}
        were free while anonymous; <strong style="color: var(--text);">{stats.stillFreeAfterReveal}</strong>{" "}
        are free now.
      </li>
      <li>
        Post-reveal input prices span{" "}
        <strong style="color: var(--text);">{stats.priceSpreadFactor}x</strong> — from $
        {stats.inputPriceFloor} to ${stats.inputPriceCeiling} per million tokens.
      </li>
      <li>
        The gaps between codenames so far are{" "}
        <strong style="color: var(--text);">{stats.gaps.map((gap) => gap.days).join(" and ")} days</strong>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="measure" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
      Free to quote, chart and reprint under <strong style="color: var(--text);">{ALPHA_LINE_LICENSE}</strong>{" "}
      with a link to this page. The same rows are at{" "}
      <a href="/alpha-line-report.json">/alpha-line-report.json</a>, and the register they come from is{" "}
      <a href="/stealth-models">/stealth-models</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="measure" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
      <strong style="color: var(--text);">How to cite this:</strong> {ALPHA_LINE_CITATION}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="method">
    <h2>Method</h2>
    <ul class="measure" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
      <li>
        Every price and window on this page is read from{" "}
        <code>openrouter.ai/api/v1/models</code>, and the whole snapshot is re-checked against that
        endpoint by <code>npm run sync-models</code>, which fails on any mismatch rather than warning.
        The 2026-09-22 run is why two prices changed on this site the same day.
      </li>
      <li>
        Day counts are whole days between the two ISO dates in the table, computed rather than typed.
      </li>
      <li>
        The disclosure date for Hunter Alpha comes from our own record of that release; for OX Alpha we
        never had a clickable source, and the cell is empty rather than estimated. Union Alpha's reveal
        is documented on OpenRouter's own stealth page.
      </li>
      <li>
        The register itself — one row per codename, with the fields you can re-check — is{" "}
        <a href="/stealth-models">/stealth-models</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="faq">
    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <Faq items={faqs} />
  </section>

  <section class="card" style="margin-top: var(--space-12);">
    <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">Get told when the next codename appears</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure);">
      The line has produced {stats.count} codenames since March 2026 and the gaps are closing. Leave an
      email and we will send one note when the next anonymous model shows up in the catalogue, with the
      fields read from the listing.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--space-4);">
      <a class="btn primary" href="/#subscribe">Subscribe</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/stealth-models">The register →</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</System>
